# Contributing with an agent, under the ladder

### The repository's guardrails, and your own fence

Tutors Ladder, Summit, talk 2

---

## The Summit has no Rung Fence

- Every variant fenced your agent to its rung
- tutors-mono-repo has no such fence: an agent can reach every package, workflow and baseline
- It has strong guardrails of its own, mostly at L3 and L4
- At the pinned commit it has **no `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md`**: little at L2 for an agent to read first

So you bring two things: the repository's gates, and a fence of your own.

---

## What the repository gives an agent to read (L2)

| File | What it tells |
| --- | --- |
| `CONTRIBUTING.md` | setup, the three local checks, what CI checks, EARS Rules, changelog hints |
| `docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md` | one page from URL to pixels (also an L1 worked path) |
| `guides/testing/TESTING-OVERVIEW.md` | the test tiers and every baseline |
| `.claude/skills/ears-gherkin-dev/SKILL.md` | a Claude Code skill: eight steps for a behavioural change, spec first |
| `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` | the checklist your PR must fill in |

None is under 500 words or loaded at the start of every session. Your session manifest fills that gap.

---

## Checked and explained (L3, L4): ratchets

"Baselines only shrink" (`guides/testing/TESTING-OVERVIEW.md`):

- a problem not in the baseline fails the build
- a baseline line that no longer occurs **also** fails the build
- so fixing something means deleting its line

`tests/architecture/known-violations.txt` holds the dependency-cruiser violations that predate the rules.
`tests/architecture/dependency-rules.test.ts` reads it. Eight more baselines work the same way.

---

## Checked: EARS Rules as the requirement

- Behaviour a student, lecturer or operator can observe needs a `Rule:` in `tests/bdd/features`
- EARS form, exactly one "shall", a `@rule-NNNN` id and an `@ears-*` tag
- Bound to steps that exercise product code; `pnpm test:bdd` runs them
- `pnpm test:ears:audit` checks their shape; `--next-id` gives a free id (check open PRs too)

The skill's rule for agents: write the Rule, show it to the human, and "wait for a yes: the requirement is
the thing they are approving".

---

## Checked: floors and budgets

| Check | Surprise (from CONTRIBUTING.md) |
| --- | --- |
| coverage floors (`tests/suite-health/coverage-floors.json`) | fail when coverage drops, **and** when it sits 2 or more points above a floor |
| mutation floors (`tests/mutation/nightly-mutation-floors.json`) | a new library module with no floor fails |
| changelog hint (`changelog-hint.yml`) | a dependency change needs `sbom` in the PR title |

An agent that "improves" tests can turn CI red. Read the message: it names the file to change.

---

## The Single Gate and the Human Boundary

- **Single Gate**: a PR merges with one approving review and a green **CI success** check, which needs every
  job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
- **Human Boundary**: `.github/CODEOWNERS` puts `.github/`, `release/claims.yaml`, `release/SOP.md` and
  `SECURITY.md` behind the maintainers
- A broad release claim needs `approvedBy`: "a person, never a bot" (`release/README.md`)
- The release command stops at the reviewer's glance, "because step 8 must stay human"

---

## The release harness, from a contributor's side

You do not run the harness. You make its job possible:

- a PR title in Conventional Commits form with its artefact hint: `fix(reader): raise nav contrast (axe, dom)`
- no hint means "nothing observable should differ"
- at release time, the hint becomes a claim in `release/claims.yaml`
- an observable difference nobody claimed fails the release

---

## Your fence: a session manifest

A short file you give your agent at the start of every session. It is your L2, and it borrows the ladder's
fence.

- **Rung**: the rung this change sits on, and the upstream areas that rung mirrors
- **May touch**: the files the issue names, their tests, at most one feature file
- **Must not touch**: other packages, workflows, baselines (except deleting a fixed line), floors, claims
- **Gate**: the commands to run before saying "done"
- **Ask a human first**: any new dependency, any edited test, anything under CODEOWNERS

The lab gives you a template.

---

## Choosing a good first issue at your rung

| The issue touches | Its rung |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/jsr/gen`, `model`, `tutors` | 1 Seed |
| `packages/jsr/tutors-lite` | 2 Lite |
| `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`, `packages/jsr/types`, `tests/architecture` | 3 Layers |
| `apps/reader`, `packages/svelte/ui-*`, `utils/i18n` | 4 Reader |
| `tests/`, `tests/bdd/features`, `guides/testing` | 5 Proven |
| `utils/rbac`, `identity`, `sanitize.ts`, security headers | 6 Guarded |
| `.github/workflows`, `release/`, `Dockerfile`, `deploy/` | 7 Shipped |

Pick from the `good first issue` label. CONTRIBUTING.md: each "names the files to change, the expected result
and how to check it".

---

## Climb no higher than you have reached

- Take an issue at or below the highest rung you have finished
- If it spans two rungs, take it only if you have finished both
- Docs fixes sit at Orientation: the walkthrough drift you found in Lab 2 is one
- Say "I'll take this" on the issue before you start; that is enough for a good first issue

---

## The loop

1. Read the issue and the files it names, yourself
2. Write the session manifest; start the agent with it
3. If behaviour changes: the EARS Rule first, approved by you
4. Let the agent change code; run the gates
5. Read every line of the diff; cut what you cannot explain
6. Open the PR with the template filled in honestly

---

## Summary

- The real repository guards itself mostly at L3 and L4
- You add the L2 an agent lacks, and a fence of your own
- Pick work at your rung; climb on purpose
- The lab takes you through one contribution, end to end
